# Template for secrets/turing-api.env. Real file is gitignored.
# Holds the runtime env for the Turing API (FastAPI service in turing-api/).
#
# Unlike next.env / convex.env, this file is not pushed by `pnpm secrets:sync`
# because the Turing cluster is not a managed platform we can talk to over an
# API. Edit values here, then scp the file to the cluster:
#
#   scp secrets/turing-api.env <user>@<turing-host>:~/tom.quest/turing-api/.env

# Shared API key. Must match TURING_API_KEY in next.env and convex.env.
#
# This one key opens EVERY endpoint, including POST /sessions/{name}/run —
# arbitrary shell commands on the cluster — and it doubles as the HMAC secret
# that signs terminal WebSocket tokens. Anything holding it holds the cluster.
TURING_API_KEY=

# Optional second key, valid for GET requests only. Leave blank to disable.
#
# It exists so a caller that only wants to LOOK — a TTS session checking
# whether its job is still queued, a dashboard, an uptime probe — does not have
# to be handed the key above. The boundary is the HTTP method rather than a
# path list, because every observing endpoint here is a GET and every mutating
# one is a POST or DELETE; a route added later is therefore write-protected by
# default, whereas a path allowlist would have to be edited in lockstep and
# fails open when someone forgets.
#
# It cannot open the terminal either: ws.py verifies token HMACs against
# TURING_API_KEY alone, so a token signed with this value does not verify.
#
# MUST be a distinct value — the service refuses to start if the two are equal,
# since a "read-only" key equal to the full key silently grants writes.
TURING_READONLY_API_KEY=

# Optional: override the default port (8000).
# API_PORT=
